The static trace confirms that the claim really loads the regenerated constructor, resolves and calls its closure, evaluates `a*h` left-to-right, divides, returns, and restores the frame. The crucial trust boundary is `divII`: it is opaque to `kprove`, appears in both execution and the postcondition, and its only concrete equation is not CPython-faithful on the unrestricted integer claim. I’m running two independent discrimination probes now: one changes the executed body while preserving the original result obligation; the other changes only the postcondition.
